    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>More than 900 statues were carved, mostly from the tuff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rano-raraku">Rano Raraku</a>, where 394 in varying states of completion are still visible; hundreds were moved to ahu platforms around the island's edge.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> regarded the moai chiefly as the living faces of deified ancestors, and archaeologists suggest they were also symbols of religious and political authority charged with sacred spirit, or mana.</li><li>Every erected statue had fallen by the late 19th century, and about 50 have since been re-erected; the island's population was devastated by slave raids in 1862, and the site was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1995.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The statues had eyes: in 1979 Sergio Rapu Haoa and a team of archaeologists reassembled fragments of white coral and showed that the eye sockets held coral eyes with black obsidian or red scoria pupils.</p></aside>
    </section>

